WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.168 --> 00:00:08.158
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.158 --> 00:00:10.088
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.088 --> 00:00:12.938
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.948 --> 00:00:14.708
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.708 --> 00:00:17.408
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.418 --> 00:00:19.478
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.478 --> 00:00:21.288
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.778 --> 00:00:24.158
I'm Alastair Budge, and today

00:00:24.368 --> 00:00:26.488
it's the start of a three-part

00:00:26.488 --> 00:00:29.358
mini-series, this time on the theme

00:00:29.408 --> 00:00:32.318
of blasphemy and free expression.

00:00:33.018 --> 00:00:35.118
In part one, this episode,

00:00:35.318 --> 00:00:36.408
we'll be talking about the

00:00:36.408 --> 00:00:38.488
history of blasphemy in Britain.

00:00:39.008 --> 00:00:41.438
Parts two and three will follow the

00:00:41.448 --> 00:00:44.638
story of the most famous blasphemy case

00:00:44.808 --> 00:00:47.638
in modern British history, the story of

00:00:47.648 --> 00:00:50.638
Salman Rushdie and the Satanic Verses.

00:00:50.998 --> 00:00:53.878
It’s a story about a famous British author

00:00:54.098 --> 00:00:57.528
whose book was deemed so incendiary, so

00:00:57.538 --> 00:01:00.378
blasphemous, that the Supreme Leader of

00:01:00.388 --> 00:01:03.348
Iran issued a death sentence against him

00:01:03.618 --> 00:01:06.858
and he had to go into hiding for 10 years.

00:01:07.488 --> 00:01:09.798
OK then, let's not waste a

00:01:09.808 --> 00:01:12.328
minute and get right into it.

00:01:13.908 --> 00:01:18.188
In January 1697, a young man named

00:01:18.268 --> 00:01:21.388
Thomas Aikenhead was sitting in a prison

00:01:21.388 --> 00:01:24.238
cell in Edinburgh, waiting to die.

00:01:24.978 --> 00:01:27.508
He was twenty years old, a student

00:01:27.748 --> 00:01:29.448
at the University of Edinburgh.

00:01:30.658 --> 00:01:33.518
His crime, for which he had been tried,

00:01:33.828 --> 00:01:36.738
convicted, and sentenced to hang by

00:01:36.738 --> 00:01:39.958
his neck until dead, was blasphemy.

00:01:41.088 --> 00:01:44.378
So, what exactly had he done?

00:01:45.048 --> 00:01:47.408
Well, he had done what many students

00:01:47.408 --> 00:01:49.278
around the world still do today.

00:01:49.858 --> 00:01:51.598
He had questioned what he

00:01:51.598 --> 00:01:53.258
had been told was true.

00:01:54.108 --> 00:01:56.188
He had made remarks to friends,

00:01:56.308 --> 00:01:58.188
in conversation, at a dinner.

00:01:58.928 --> 00:02:01.178
He had called the Old Testament

00:02:01.568 --> 00:02:04.288
"Ezra's fables," suggesting that

00:02:04.288 --> 00:02:06.518
the first part of the Bible was

00:02:06.518 --> 00:02:08.548
no more reliable than a myth.

00:02:09.378 --> 00:02:13.038
He had described theology as, and I quote,

00:02:13.438 --> 00:02:16.818
“a rhapsody of ill-invented nonsense”.

00:02:17.568 --> 00:02:19.868
He had suggested, at various points,

00:02:20.078 --> 00:02:22.748
that Jesus Christ was an impostor.

00:02:23.698 --> 00:02:25.788
He had not published a pamphlet,

00:02:26.118 --> 00:02:28.648
or preached in a public square, or

00:02:28.648 --> 00:02:31.208
nailed anything to a church door.

00:02:31.798 --> 00:02:34.498
He had floated these ideas to his friends.

00:02:35.278 --> 00:02:37.088
The problem was that these

00:02:37.128 --> 00:02:39.418
ideas were blasphemous.

00:02:40.358 --> 00:02:43.354
On the 8th of January, 1697,

00:02:43.968 --> 00:02:46.198
Thomas Aikenhead was hanged.

00:02:47.008 --> 00:02:49.768
He was the last person executed

00:02:49.768 --> 00:02:51.578
for blasphemy in Britain.

00:02:52.428 --> 00:02:55.138
Now, it is tempting to think of this

00:02:55.168 --> 00:02:57.908
as a story from the distant past — the

00:02:57.908 --> 00:03:00.018
kind of thing that happened in a more

00:03:00.018 --> 00:03:02.318
primitive era, before science and

00:03:02.318 --> 00:03:04.788
reason arrived to improve the world.

00:03:05.458 --> 00:03:07.718
But the distance is not

00:03:07.758 --> 00:03:09.458
as great as it might seem.

00:03:10.111 --> 00:03:13.248
1697 is not the Middle Ages.

00:03:13.628 --> 00:03:15.158
The Bank of England had been

00:03:15.158 --> 00:03:17.148
founded just a few years earlier.

00:03:17.771 --> 00:03:20.368
Isaac Newton's theories of motion

00:03:20.408 --> 00:03:22.668
and gravitation were ten years old.

00:03:22.958 --> 00:03:24.488
John Locke had recently

00:03:24.498 --> 00:03:26.498
published his famous Essay

00:03:26.498 --> 00:03:28.098
Concerning Human Understanding.

00:03:28.738 --> 00:03:31.508
This was, by the standards of its time,

00:03:31.848 --> 00:03:35.318
a highly educated and rational society.

00:03:36.038 --> 00:03:38.038
And yet it hanged a

00:03:38.038 --> 00:03:39.608
student for conversation.

00:03:40.848 --> 00:03:44.358
That fact, and the long strange

00:03:44.358 --> 00:03:46.288
journey from that moment to the

00:03:46.298 --> 00:03:48.808
present, is what this episode is about.

00:03:49.488 --> 00:03:52.708
So, to go back to its origins,

00:03:53.028 --> 00:03:56.348
“blasphemy” is a very old word, and

00:03:56.638 --> 00:03:58.638
where it comes from is important.

00:03:58.948 --> 00:04:01.874
It comes from the Greek, blasphemia.

00:04:02.238 --> 00:04:06.058
The root blapt means to injure; and

00:04:06.058 --> 00:04:08.888
pheme means speech or reputation.

00:04:09.738 --> 00:04:12.268
Blasphemia, then, means

00:04:12.388 --> 00:04:15.068
roughly to speak harmfully of

00:04:15.068 --> 00:04:17.595
something, or to speak ill of it.

00:04:18.588 --> 00:04:22.378
And in its original Greek sense, it

00:04:22.378 --> 00:04:25.118
did not specifically mean speaking

00:04:25.158 --> 00:04:27.868
against God, or a religious deity.

00:04:28.348 --> 00:04:30.818
It meant speaking harmfully about

00:04:31.148 --> 00:04:34.948
anyone or anything held in high regard.

00:04:35.338 --> 00:04:37.598
You could blaspheme a person,

00:04:37.958 --> 00:04:39.958
a king, or a reputation.

00:04:41.148 --> 00:04:43.528
When the word passed into

00:04:43.628 --> 00:04:45.918
English through Latin and Old

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:48.378
French, it had already changed.

00:04:49.228 --> 00:04:52.568
Blasphemy now meant, specifically,

00:04:52.988 --> 00:04:55.298
speaking against God, against

00:04:55.348 --> 00:04:57.608
sacred things, against the

00:04:57.608 --> 00:04:59.328
teachings of the Church.

00:05:00.168 --> 00:05:03.348
And the reason this was treated as a

00:05:03.358 --> 00:05:06.978
serious crime was not simply religious.

00:05:07.208 --> 00:05:08.248
It was political.

00:05:09.418 --> 00:05:12.418
In a society where the Church and

00:05:12.418 --> 00:05:14.648
the state were deeply connected,

00:05:15.078 --> 00:05:17.968
speaking against God was speaking

00:05:17.968 --> 00:05:19.968
against the order of the world.

00:05:20.658 --> 00:05:23.888
A king ruled by divine right.

00:05:24.308 --> 00:05:27.288
Courts relied on Christian oaths.

00:05:27.498 --> 00:05:30.288
The law itself was intertwined

00:05:30.518 --> 00:05:32.258
with Christian assumptions

00:05:32.418 --> 00:05:34.608
about morality and order.

00:05:35.743 --> 00:05:37.983
And this was made even more

00:05:37.983 --> 00:05:40.963
explicit in the XVI century

00:05:41.223 --> 00:05:43.710
when Henry VIII broke with Rome.

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.070
When he made himself the head

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:48.452
of the Church of England,

00:05:48.832 --> 00:05:51.442
religious authority and political

00:05:51.492 --> 00:05:54.392
authority became one and the same.

00:05:54.802 --> 00:05:57.632
The Crown and the Church were fused.

00:05:59.142 --> 00:06:02.142
This meant that to challenge the Church

00:06:02.472 --> 00:06:04.972
was, effectively, to challenge the Crown.

00:06:05.552 --> 00:06:09.122
And that made blasphemy not just a

00:06:09.132 --> 00:06:12.332
sin, but something closer to rebellion.

00:06:13.355 --> 00:06:16.425
The law that developed around this

00:06:16.485 --> 00:06:19.735
was never tidy — there was no single

00:06:19.875 --> 00:06:21.475
act of Parliament that defined

00:06:21.475 --> 00:06:25.105
it — but its purpose was consistent.

00:06:25.455 --> 00:06:27.985
It protected the established Church

00:06:27.995 --> 00:06:30.725
of England and, by extension, the

00:06:30.725 --> 00:06:33.088
political order that rested upon it.

00:06:33.895 --> 00:06:35.314
That point will become

00:06:35.364 --> 00:06:37.004
very important later on.

00:06:37.764 --> 00:06:40.804
But set aside, for a moment, the question

00:06:40.804 --> 00:06:43.284
of which church the law was protecting.

00:06:43.904 --> 00:06:46.594
There is a more basic question worth

00:06:46.594 --> 00:06:50.084
asking: why did it matter so much at all?

00:06:50.524 --> 00:06:53.314
Why would a supposedly confident

00:06:53.374 --> 00:06:56.094
Christian society care so much

00:06:56.244 --> 00:06:58.374
about a few offensive words?

00:06:59.264 --> 00:07:01.874
Of course, people genuinely believed

00:07:01.904 --> 00:07:04.884
that the sacred mattered, and that they

00:07:04.884 --> 00:07:07.542
had a Christian duty to protect it.

00:07:08.128 --> 00:07:10.894
But private doubt was

00:07:10.904 --> 00:07:12.694
never the real target.

00:07:13.414 --> 00:07:16.714
What the law cared about was public

00:07:16.774 --> 00:07:19.434
challenge: the pamphlet, the printing

00:07:19.434 --> 00:07:22.254
press, the speech in a public square.

00:07:23.234 --> 00:07:27.024
And those who faced the consequences were

00:07:27.024 --> 00:07:30.354
those who dared to challenge most openly.

00:07:31.471 --> 00:07:34.114
Percy Bysshe Shelley is a good example.

00:07:34.774 --> 00:07:37.054
Today he is remembered as one of

00:07:37.074 --> 00:07:39.384
England's greatest Romantic poets.

00:07:39.714 --> 00:07:43.494
But in 1811, he was expelled from Oxford

00:07:43.494 --> 00:07:46.474
University for publishing a short pamphlet

00:07:46.704 --> 00:07:48.624
called "The Necessity of Atheism."

00:07:49.494 --> 00:07:52.264
He and his co-author had sent the

00:07:52.264 --> 00:07:54.304
pamphlet to the heads of Oxford

00:07:54.304 --> 00:07:56.724
colleges and to several bishops,

00:07:57.114 --> 00:07:59.614
apparently hoping to start a debate.

00:08:00.124 --> 00:08:01.524
What they started was a

00:08:01.564 --> 00:08:04.034
disciplinary hearing, and they

00:08:04.034 --> 00:08:06.014
were expelled the same afternoon.

00:08:06.811 --> 00:08:10.651
And if Shelley shows what institutional

00:08:10.721 --> 00:08:12.421
punishment could look like, the

00:08:12.421 --> 00:08:14.681
experience of a man called Richard

00:08:14.721 --> 00:08:17.461
Carlile shows what happened when the

00:08:17.511 --> 00:08:20.291
criminal law was used more directly.

00:08:20.861 --> 00:08:23.291
Carlile was a printer and political

00:08:23.301 --> 00:08:25.981
journalist who spent years in prison in

00:08:25.981 --> 00:08:29.181
the 1820s for publishing works considered

00:08:29.181 --> 00:08:31.591
blasphemous, including Thomas Paine's

00:08:31.601 --> 00:08:33.941
"The Age of Reason," which is a critique

00:08:33.971 --> 00:08:36.331
of the Bible and organised religion.

00:08:37.021 --> 00:08:38.821
While Carlile was in prison,

00:08:39.141 --> 00:08:41.241
his wife and sister kept running

00:08:41.241 --> 00:08:42.491
the publishing shop in London.

00:08:43.131 --> 00:08:45.661
They were also charged and imprisoned.

00:08:46.071 --> 00:08:47.911
Then volunteers came

00:08:47.911 --> 00:08:48.881
to keep the shop open.

00:08:49.341 --> 00:08:51.001
They were imprisoned too.

00:08:52.331 --> 00:08:55.201
Carlile argued, explicitly, that

00:08:55.211 --> 00:08:57.891
blasphemy law was a political tool.

00:08:58.401 --> 00:09:00.511
It was not being used to protect

00:09:00.541 --> 00:09:03.351
God; it was being used to protect

00:09:03.371 --> 00:09:05.721
the ruling class, which had built

00:09:05.741 --> 00:09:07.701
its authority on the claim that

00:09:07.721 --> 00:09:10.241
the social order was God's design.

00:09:10.791 --> 00:09:13.771
To question the Bible was, in this

00:09:13.771 --> 00:09:16.251
reading, to question why some people

00:09:16.441 --> 00:09:18.871
owned land and others did not.

00:09:19.941 --> 00:09:22.721
Now, by the twentieth century,

00:09:23.151 --> 00:09:26.091
most people in Britain assumed that

00:09:26.091 --> 00:09:28.711
blasphemy law was effectively dead.

00:09:29.241 --> 00:09:31.751
It hadn't been enforced in decades.

00:09:32.261 --> 00:09:34.651
Religious belief was in decline.

00:09:35.161 --> 00:09:37.451
Satirical publications were more

00:09:37.451 --> 00:09:40.151
than happy to poke fun at the church.

00:09:40.721 --> 00:09:43.421
The general feeling was that, as a

00:09:43.421 --> 00:09:46.411
society, we had moved past all that.

00:09:47.341 --> 00:09:52.201
In 1977, a woman named Mary Whitehouse

00:09:52.401 --> 00:09:54.291
proved that was not the case.

00:09:54.921 --> 00:09:57.871
Whitehouse was a campaigner for what she

00:09:57.871 --> 00:10:00.991
called moral standards in public life.

00:10:01.631 --> 00:10:03.661
She had spent years challenging

00:10:03.841 --> 00:10:07.431
what she saw as the moral decline of

00:10:07.461 --> 00:10:09.511
British television and publishing.

00:10:10.571 --> 00:10:14.781
In 1977, she brought a private prosecution

00:10:15.131 --> 00:10:18.001
against a newspaper called Gay News

00:10:18.291 --> 00:10:20.631
for publishing a poem called "The

00:10:20.631 --> 00:10:22.981
Love That Dares to Speak Its Name."

00:10:24.081 --> 00:10:25.791
The poem was written by

00:10:25.791 --> 00:10:27.611
the academic James Kirkup.

00:10:28.371 --> 00:10:30.661
Now, you can find this poem online,

00:10:31.041 --> 00:10:32.711
but I have to warn you, it is quite

00:10:32.711 --> 00:10:35.901
explicit and not for the easily offended.

00:10:36.821 --> 00:10:39.261
It is written from the perspective

00:10:39.381 --> 00:10:42.081
of a homosexual Roman soldier who

00:10:42.081 --> 00:10:44.321
had been at the crucifixion, and

00:10:44.561 --> 00:10:47.161
it describes his erotic feelings

00:10:47.451 --> 00:10:49.461
towards the body of Christ.

00:10:50.481 --> 00:10:52.661
Whitehouse argued this

00:10:52.661 --> 00:10:54.531
was blasphemous libel.

00:10:55.691 --> 00:10:58.601
The case went to trial at the Old Bailey,

00:10:58.881 --> 00:11:01.291
England's most famous criminal court.

00:11:02.171 --> 00:11:04.181
The editor of Gay News,

00:11:04.411 --> 00:11:06.591
Denis Lemon, was convicted.

00:11:07.131 --> 00:11:08.941
He received a suspended

00:11:08.991 --> 00:11:10.951
prison sentence and a fine.

00:11:11.681 --> 00:11:13.741
This was 1977.

00:11:14.231 --> 00:11:16.261
The year of the Sex Pistols

00:11:16.411 --> 00:11:18.252
and the Queen's Silver Jubilee.

00:11:18.878 --> 00:11:21.771
And yet a newspaper editor had been

00:11:21.771 --> 00:11:24.151
convicted for publishing a poem,

00:11:24.491 --> 00:11:26.771
under a law whose roots stretched

00:11:26.771 --> 00:11:28.951
back nearly three centuries.

00:11:29.921 --> 00:11:32.121
The reaction was fierce.

00:11:32.741 --> 00:11:34.511
For many people, especially

00:11:34.511 --> 00:11:36.591
in journalism and the arts,

00:11:36.971 --> 00:11:38.841
the conviction was an outrage.

00:11:39.271 --> 00:11:41.461
A poem was not a crime.

00:11:41.831 --> 00:11:44.101
Freedom of expression had to

00:11:44.101 --> 00:11:46.131
include the right to offend.

00:11:47.131 --> 00:11:49.921
For Whitehouse and her supporters, it was

00:11:49.921 --> 00:11:52.791
a statement that some things were beyond

00:11:52.801 --> 00:11:55.171
what a decent society should tolerate.

00:11:56.381 --> 00:11:59.951
And two years later, in 1979, Monty

00:11:59.951 --> 00:12:02.521
Python released Life of Brian.

00:12:03.571 --> 00:12:07.351
The film follows Brian of Nazareth, a man

00:12:07.621 --> 00:12:11.171
born in a stable next door to Jesus, who

00:12:11.171 --> 00:12:14.171
is constantly mistaken for the Messiah.

00:12:14.981 --> 00:12:16.571
The Church of England and the

00:12:16.571 --> 00:12:18.401
Catholic Church condemned it.

00:12:18.951 --> 00:12:20.651
Several local councils in

00:12:20.651 --> 00:12:22.571
Britain banned it outright.

00:12:23.321 --> 00:12:25.711
Norway banned it altogether,

00:12:26.081 --> 00:12:28.081
which Swedish cinema posters

00:12:28.101 --> 00:12:30.831
exploited cheerfully, writing "So

00:12:30.831 --> 00:12:32.781
funny it was banned in Norway."

00:12:33.581 --> 00:12:37.091
Now, Life of Brian was never prosecuted.

00:12:37.521 --> 00:12:40.721
But the Gay News case and the Life

00:12:40.751 --> 00:12:43.881
of Brian row showed that, in Britain

00:12:44.131 --> 00:12:46.991
in the late 1970s, the question of

00:12:46.991 --> 00:12:49.521
what could and couldn't be said about

00:12:49.521 --> 00:12:52.111
religion was still very much alive.

00:12:53.181 --> 00:12:55.051
And then, a decade later,

00:12:55.501 --> 00:12:56.781
everything shifted.

00:12:57.901 --> 00:13:01.351
In September 1988, Salman

00:13:01.361 --> 00:13:03.341
Rushdie published a novel

00:13:03.401 --> 00:13:05.401
called The Satanic Verses.

00:13:06.491 --> 00:13:08.781
The novel caused immediate and

00:13:08.791 --> 00:13:11.121
widespread offence among Muslim

00:13:11.121 --> 00:13:12.601
communities around the world.

00:13:13.151 --> 00:13:15.171
The full story of that controversy

00:13:15.431 --> 00:13:17.211
is what we'll cover in the next two

00:13:17.211 --> 00:13:19.631
episodes, so you’ll forgive me for

00:13:19.641 --> 00:13:21.451
only mentioning it in passing here.

00:13:22.033 --> 00:13:25.603
But one consequence of the protests

00:13:25.633 --> 00:13:28.973
and this uproar matters directly

00:13:29.163 --> 00:13:31.373
for the history of blasphemy law.

00:13:32.423 --> 00:13:34.413
When Muslim organisations in

00:13:34.423 --> 00:13:37.283
Britain looked for legal protection

00:13:37.513 --> 00:13:39.583
against the book, naturally,

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:41.963
they turned to blasphemy law.

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:44.310
And here they discovered

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:45.940
something remarkable.

00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:49.160
The law was not available to them.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:52.080
English blasphemy law, as

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:54.030
it had developed over three

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:56.850
centuries, protected Christianity,

00:13:57.390 --> 00:13:59.340
specifically the Church of England.

00:13:59.930 --> 00:14:02.400
It had never been extended to cover

00:14:02.590 --> 00:14:05.960
Islam, or Judaism, or any other faith.

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:09.200
This was not a technicality.

00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:13.280
It revealed the real character of the law.

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:16.580
It had never been a neutral law

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:18.890
protecting the sacred as such.

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:21.900
It was a structural feature of a law

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:24.530
that had grown up as the legal arm

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:26.680
of an established Christian state.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:29.980
Still, the implication

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:32.170
was deeply uncomfortable.

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:35.490
British law was telling Muslim

00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:38.450
citizens that their faith deserved

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:40.920
less legal protection than the

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:42.900
faith of their Christian neighbours.

00:14:43.790 --> 00:14:46.530
For communities already dealing with

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:50.020
racism, economic disadvantages, and

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:52.820
often a sense that mainstream British

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:56.000
society didn't understand them, this

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:58.510
was more than a legal disappointment.

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:00.240
It confirmed something

00:15:00.510 --> 00:15:02.620
many had long suspected.

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:05.760
And the debate that followed

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:08.210
was long and difficult.

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:10.840
Should blasphemy law be

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:13.120
extended to cover all religions?

00:15:13.590 --> 00:15:16.250
That would give equal protection to Islam,

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:19.090
Judaism, Hinduism, and every other faith.

00:15:19.863 --> 00:15:22.460
But it would also make it far harder

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:25.790
to criticise or satirise any of them.

00:15:26.980 --> 00:15:30.590
Or should the law be abolished altogether?

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:34.390
The argument for abolition was that

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:37.490
in a largely secular society with

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:39.510
many different religions and beliefs,

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:42.750
no set of ideas — however important

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:44.810
to those who hold them — can be

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:46.960
protected from challenge by law.

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:49.720
You can protect people.

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:52.050
You cannot protect ideas.

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:55.744
Parliament eventually chose abolition.

00:15:56.420 --> 00:15:58.350
The Criminal Justice and Immigration

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:01.970
Act of 2008 ended blasphemy as a

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:03.980
criminal offence in England and Wales.

00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:05.570
The three-hundred-year

00:16:05.580 --> 00:16:07.240
experiment was over.

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:10.890
And of course, Britain was

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:13.010
not and has not been having

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:15.010
these discussions in isolation.

00:16:15.690 --> 00:16:18.420
The Danish cartoons controversy, and

00:16:18.430 --> 00:16:21.846
later the murders at Charlie Hebdo, were

00:16:21.866 --> 00:16:25.166
reminders that even where blasphemy is

00:16:25.166 --> 00:16:28.036
no longer a crime, offence to religion

00:16:28.306 --> 00:16:30.756
can still have enormous political

00:16:30.896 --> 00:16:32.846
and sometimes deadly consequences.

00:16:33.966 --> 00:16:36.776
And beyond Britain, the legal

00:16:36.776 --> 00:16:38.676
picture is still very uneven.

00:16:39.516 --> 00:16:41.786
In some countries, especially

00:16:42.036 --> 00:16:44.596
Muslim-majority countries, blasphemy

00:16:44.596 --> 00:16:47.476
laws remain harsh, and in a few

00:16:47.496 --> 00:16:49.656
cases can carry the death penalty.

00:16:50.456 --> 00:16:53.326
In others, there may be no formal

00:16:53.336 --> 00:16:56.416
blasphemy law as such, but there

00:16:56.416 --> 00:16:58.356
are still laws against insulting

00:16:58.356 --> 00:17:00.816
religion, offending religious feelings,

00:17:01.026 --> 00:17:02.876
or disturbing religious peace.

00:17:03.916 --> 00:17:07.226
Now, bringing it back to Britain, you

00:17:07.226 --> 00:17:09.266
might think that abolishing blasphemy

00:17:09.266 --> 00:17:12.156
law in 2008 settled the question.

00:17:13.216 --> 00:17:13.796
It didn't.

00:17:14.676 --> 00:17:18.446
In November 2024 — less than two years

00:17:18.446 --> 00:17:22.476
ago — a Labour MP named Tahir Ali stood

00:17:22.476 --> 00:17:24.956
up at Prime Minister's Questions and

00:17:25.046 --> 00:17:27.636
asked the Prime Minister, Keir Starmer,

00:17:27.966 --> 00:17:30.826
to introduce laws prohibiting the

00:17:30.836 --> 00:17:33.506
"desecration" of religious texts and

00:17:33.506 --> 00:17:35.676
the prophets of the Abrahamic religions.

00:17:36.566 --> 00:17:39.076
In other words:  a new blasphemy law.

00:17:40.226 --> 00:17:41.816
The timing was deliberate.

00:17:42.506 --> 00:17:45.286
Ali was speaking during Islamophobia

00:17:45.326 --> 00:17:48.386
Awareness Month, and his target was clear

00:17:48.866 --> 00:17:51.206
— protection for Islam, in particular,

00:17:51.576 --> 00:17:53.916
from public mockery and insult.

00:17:54.496 --> 00:17:56.666
And specifically, making burning

00:17:56.676 --> 00:17:58.686
the Quran a criminal act.

00:18:00.303 --> 00:18:01.886
Starmer's initial response

00:18:02.316 --> 00:18:04.226
did not include a clear no.

00:18:04.866 --> 00:18:06.686
He said that "desecration is

00:18:06.746 --> 00:18:08.856
awful" and that his government

00:18:08.866 --> 00:18:11.236
was "committed to tackling all

00:18:11.236 --> 00:18:13.166
forms of hatred and division."

00:18:14.626 --> 00:18:17.906
This non-answer caused immediate

00:18:17.956 --> 00:18:19.746
alarm, because it stopped

00:18:19.756 --> 00:18:21.886
short of a clear rejection.

00:18:22.716 --> 00:18:25.766
The National Secular Society wrote to the

00:18:25.766 --> 00:18:28.446
government to demand a clear rejection.

00:18:29.753 --> 00:18:32.103
Journalists and commentators across the

00:18:32.103 --> 00:18:34.893
political spectrum expressed concern.

00:18:35.673 --> 00:18:37.603
A few weeks later, the government

00:18:37.693 --> 00:18:38.913
clarified its position.

00:18:39.393 --> 00:18:41.563
A minister stated that the government

00:18:41.683 --> 00:18:43.433
would in fact "completely rule

00:18:43.433 --> 00:18:45.933
out" re-introducing blasphemy laws.

00:18:46.773 --> 00:18:50.223
So, to wrap things up, for centuries,

00:18:50.553 --> 00:18:53.683
blasphemy law in Britain was never really

00:18:53.703 --> 00:18:56.023
about protecting all faith equally.

00:18:56.423 --> 00:18:58.603
It was about protecting the religious

00:18:58.613 --> 00:19:00.583
foundations of public order.

00:19:01.343 --> 00:19:03.823
And once Britain stopped being a

00:19:03.833 --> 00:19:07.093
uniformly Christian society, these laws

00:19:07.483 --> 00:19:11.343
began to look not only old, but unfair.

00:19:12.363 --> 00:19:14.683
But still, questions about religious

00:19:14.703 --> 00:19:17.283
protection are as present as ever.

00:19:18.043 --> 00:19:20.623
Where does criticism of a religion

00:19:20.673 --> 00:19:23.013
end, and where does incitement

00:19:23.143 --> 00:19:24.673
against its followers begin?

00:19:25.363 --> 00:19:27.363
How much offence must a free

00:19:27.413 --> 00:19:29.413
society be willing to tolerate?

00:19:29.813 --> 00:19:31.883
And who gets to decide?

00:19:32.603 --> 00:19:34.743
These are old questions, but

00:19:34.983 --> 00:19:36.523
they have not stayed in the past.

00:19:36.853 --> 00:19:38.153
The language has changed.

00:19:38.463 --> 00:19:39.523
The laws have changed.

00:19:39.873 --> 00:19:41.403
Britain itself has changed.

00:19:42.023 --> 00:19:44.333
Fortunately, my country no longer

00:19:44.343 --> 00:19:46.483
hangs students for saying the wrong

00:19:46.513 --> 00:19:49.033
things at dinner, but we still

00:19:49.033 --> 00:19:51.533
has not fully settled how a free

00:19:51.533 --> 00:19:54.253
society should respond to the sacred.

00:19:55.883 --> 00:19:58.653
OK then, that is it for today's episode

00:19:58.803 --> 00:20:01.313
on the history of blasphemy in Britain.

00:20:01.873 --> 00:20:04.063
As a reminder, this is part one

00:20:04.293 --> 00:20:06.023
of a three-part mini-series.

00:20:06.313 --> 00:20:09.443
Next up, in part two and three, we

00:20:09.443 --> 00:20:11.953
tell the story of Salman Rushdie and

00:20:11.953 --> 00:20:14.403
The Satanic Verses — the book itself,

00:20:14.423 --> 00:20:16.953
the protests, the fatwa, the public

00:20:16.953 --> 00:20:19.213
debate, the assassination attempts,

00:20:19.453 --> 00:20:21.003
and the questions it got people

00:20:21.023 --> 00:20:23.223
asking about freedom of expression.

00:20:23.843 --> 00:20:25.683
You've been listening to English Learning

00:20:25.683 --> 00:20:28.243
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:20:28.553 --> 00:20:31.303
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:20:31.303 --> 00:20:33.513
I'll catch you in the next episode.

